{"date": "2026-11-18", "post_id": 1512288, "title": "A Medium-Sized Solar Flare", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-18-an-m-class-solar-flare/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> What a <a href=\"https://idioms.thefreedictionary.com/flair+for+the+dramatic\">flare for the chromatic</a>! <a href=\"http://galaxlux.com/Sun-%20Solar%20Limb%20Dynamics%2014%20May%202025.htm\">Today\u2019s image</a> captures a piece of the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/\">Sun</a>\u2019s <a href=\"https://scied.ucar.edu/learning-zone/sun-space-weather/chromosphere\">chromosphere</a>, the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/the-sun-spot/2023/09/26/layers-of-the-sun/\">layer of the Sun</a>\u2019s atmosphere above its surface where the temperature <em>rises</em>. Why would the Sun become hotter farther away from its <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/sun-heat/en/\">core</a>? This is potentially due to energy from the swirling <a href=\"https://solarscience.msfc.nasa.gov/the_key.shtml\">magnetic fields</a> extending up through the Sun\u2019s atmosphere. Those same magnetic fields can <a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/20310#media_group_320369\">twist and snap</a>, releasing light and energetic particles as a <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oOXVZo7KikE\">solar flare</a>. The <a href=\"https://skyandtelescope.org/stargazing-and-observing/guide-to-observing-the-sun-in-h-alpha092321050923/\">H-alpha filter</a> used for today\u2019s image captures light from energized hydrogen atoms as they travel off the Sun\u2019s surface during a flare on May 14, 2025. This flare was <a href=\"https://solar-center.stanford.edu/sid/activities/flare.html\">classified</a> as an M1 based on <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/goes/\">GOES</a> <a href=\"https://www.spaceweatherlive.com/en/archive/2025/05/14/xray.html\">observations</a>, meaning it was moderately bright in <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/11_xrays/\">X-rays</a>. M-class flares can cause <a href=\"https://www.spaceweather.gov/phenomena/solar-flares-radio-blackouts\">radio blackouts</a> at Earth\u2019s poles and minor <a href=\"https://www.spaceweather.gov/phenomena/solar-radiation-storm\">radiation storms</a> that can harm astronauts. They aren\u2019t even <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2018-september-2-a-powerful-solar-flare/\">the most powerful flares</a>! NASA, <a href=\"https://www.noaa.gov/\">NOAA</a>, and other agencies monitor the Sun to predict and identify such storms so we can protect humans on Earth and in space.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> a horse of a different color", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/mario.cogo.9\">Mario Cogo</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/mario.cogo.9\">Mario Cogo</a>", "alt": "A piece of the Sun takes up half the image. The Sun looks fuzzy and swirling with energetic hydrogen atoms. A bright and dark spot are visible on the Sun, associated with a sun spot. A wisp of hydrogen reaches off of the edge of the Sun.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-18-an-m-class-solar-flare/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/solar_M_flare.jpg?w=3000&h=2759&fit=clip", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 18 - A Medium-Sized Solar Flare</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-18-an-m-class-solar-flare/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: What a flare for the chromatic! Today\u2019s image captures a piece of the Sun\u2019s chromosphere, the layer of the Sun\u2019s atmosphere above its surface where the temperature rises. Why would the Sun become hotter farther away from its core? This is potentially due to energy from the swirling magnetic fields extending up through the Sun\u2019s atmosphere. Those same magnetic fields can twist and snap, releasing light and energetic particles as a solar flare. The H-alpha filter used for today\u2019s image captures light from energized hydrogen atoms as they travel off the Sun\u2019s surface during a flare on May 14, 2025. This flare was classified as an M1 based on GOES observations, meaning it was moderately bright in X-rays. M-class flares can cause radio blackouts at Earth\u2019s poles and minor radiation storms that can harm astronauts. They aren\u2019t even the most powerful flares! NASA, NOAA, and other agencies monitor the Sun to predict and identify such storms so we can protect humans on Earth and in space.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: a horse of a different color\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"a medium-sized solar flare\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"A Medium-Sized Solar Flare\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-18-an-m-class-solar-flare/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/solar_M_flare.jpg?w=3000&#038;h=2759&#038;fit=clip\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/solar_M_flare.jpg?w=3000&#038;h=2759&#038;fit=clip\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A piece of the Sun takes up half the image. The Sun looks fuzzy and swirling with energetic hydrogen atoms. A bright and dark spot are visible on the Sun, associated with a sun spot. A wisp of hydrogen reaches off of the edge of the Sun.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-18T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-17T19:20:40-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"A Medium-Sized Solar Flare\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/solar_M_flare.jpg?w=3000&#038;h=2759&#038;fit=clip\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 18<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/solar_M_flare.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/solar_M_flare.jpg\" alt=\"A piece of the Sun takes up half the image. The Sun looks fuzzy and swirling with energetic hydrogen atoms. A bright and dark spot are visible on the Sun, associated with a sun spot. A wisp of hydrogen reaches off of the edge of the Sun.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> A Medium-Sized Solar Flare </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/mario.cogo.9\">Mario Cogo</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> What a <a href=\"https://idioms.thefreedictionary.com/flair+for+the+dramatic\">flare for the chromatic</a>! <a href=\"http://galaxlux.com/Sun-%20Solar%20Limb%20Dynamics%2014%20May%202025.htm\">Today\u2019s image</a> captures a piece of the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/\">Sun</a>\u2019s <a href=\"https://scied.ucar.edu/learning-zone/sun-space-weather/chromosphere\">chromosphere</a>, the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/the-sun-spot/2023/09/26/layers-of-the-sun/\">layer of the Sun</a>\u2019s atmosphere above its surface where the temperature <em>rises</em>. Why would the Sun become hotter farther away from its <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/sun-heat/en/\">core</a>? This is potentially due to energy from the swirling <a href=\"https://solarscience.msfc.nasa.gov/the_key.shtml\">magnetic fields</a> extending up through the Sun\u2019s atmosphere. Those same magnetic fields can <a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/20310#media_group_320369\">twist and snap</a>, releasing light and energetic particles as a <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oOXVZo7KikE\">solar flare</a>. The <a href=\"https://skyandtelescope.org/stargazing-and-observing/guide-to-observing-the-sun-in-h-alpha092321050923/\">H-alpha filter</a> used for today\u2019s image captures light from energized hydrogen atoms as they travel off the Sun\u2019s surface during a flare on May 14, 2025. This flare was <a href=\"https://solar-center.stanford.edu/sid/activities/flare.html\">classified</a> as an M1 based on <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/goes/\">GOES</a> <a href=\"https://www.spaceweatherlive.com/en/archive/2025/05/14/xray.html\">observations</a>, meaning it was moderately bright in <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/11_xrays/\">X-rays</a>. M-class flares can cause <a href=\"https://www.spaceweather.gov/phenomena/solar-flares-radio-blackouts\">radio blackouts</a> at Earth\u2019s poles and minor <a href=\"https://www.spaceweather.gov/phenomena/solar-radiation-storm\">radiation storms</a> that can harm astronauts. They aren\u2019t even <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2018-september-2-a-powerful-solar-flare/\">the most powerful flares</a>! NASA, <a href=\"https://www.noaa.gov/\">NOAA</a>, and other agencies monitor the Sun to predict and identify such storms so we can protect humans on Earth and in space.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> a horse of a different color\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-17-milky-way-versus-aurora/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nKeighley Rockcliffe, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261118/html"}